# af-spec

You write a specification package — the four files of format version 2 — with
a person watching. It is the middle of three steps: `af-prd` writes the PRD,
this skill turns it into a package, `af-impl` implements it. The `spec`
command does the same work in one unattended model run; this skill does it in
the session, so that the person reads each artifact as it appears and the
code the package is written against is the code you have just read. Where the
two differ in method, `spec`'s pipeline is right: it implements the format's
generation contract (§12 of `spec-format-v2.md`), and so does everything
below.

Five rules shape it. **The plan is the product**: every criterion is verified
by a test, every test is owned by a task, every execution path is exercised
by a smoke test, and a package that breaks one of those is not a draft to
improve later — it is invalid. **One order, one gate**: `requirements.json`,
then `test_spec.json`, then `tasks.json`, each written against the complete
text of the ones before it, each followed by `speca --validate`. **The
verdict is the format's**: `speca` decides whether the package is valid, not
your reading of it; you fix what it names and ask again. **Draft until it
validates**: the package is written in `draft` and activated only on a clean
verdict, because activation hashes the intent and closes the package to
edits. **Behaviour from the PRD, names from the code**: what the system must
do comes from the PRD and nothing else; the files, commands, types and test
commands the package names are the ones the repository really has.

Two commands from the `spec` repository do the bookkeeping, and each writes
one JSON object to stdout: `speca` reports a package's state and gives the
format's verdict on it, and `specr` renders a package as Markdown. Read their
JSON; do not work out a package's number, scope or verdict by reading files
by hand while the commands are there to ask. `make build` in a checkout of
`github.com/agent-fox-dev/spec` installs both. Without them, §10 says what to
do instead, and the report says you did.

If `.specs/steering.md` exists, read it first and let it answer every
question it answers — it is the project's own directive to every agent, and
`spec`'s phases are given it too. Follow the repository's own agent
instructions (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`) for where documents live, numbering
and commits; where they say nothing, the defaults below apply.

## How the run goes

One direction: input, orientation, the number, the size, the PRD, the three
artifacts in order, the verdict, activation, the report. You stop for the
person's answers in §1 and §4 and for nothing else. Do not ask whether to
start an artifact, whether a requirement reads well, or whether to go on to
the next file. Show each artifact as you write it; the person steers by
answering and interrupting.

You stop short of a written package, and say why, for these reasons only:

- the idea is not yet a PRD and the person wants one written properly first
  (§1): `af-prd` is that step;
- the PRD's intent is already an active or sealed package's intent, and the
  person has not said what this package changes (§2);
- the work does not fit one package and the person has not chosen the scope
  to write first (§4);
- `speca` refuses to read the spec root, or the spec root is not writable.

Everything else is information, not a stop: a dependency that is not sealed,
a warning, a repair loop, a PRD with a gap you can close from the code.

## 1. Read the input

`$ARGUMENTS` is one of:

- **a path to a PRD** — the normal case, and what `af-prd` leaves behind. Its
  body is the intent of the package and the source of every requirement;
- **a path to a draft package** (`.specs/17_name`, or `17`) — a package to
  finish or repair. Read it with `specr`, take its verdict (§10), and write
  only what is missing or broken. An `active` or `sealed` package is not
  this: say so and stop (§2);
- **an idea in prose, or an issue URL** — no PRD exists yet. Read the issue
  and its comments where it is one (with `gh` or `glab` when installed). Say,
  in one line, that `/af-prd` writes the PRD with them and that this run can
  instead write the PRD itself, deciding the open questions and recording
  each in `## Design Decisions`, and let them choose. If they choose this
  run, write the body of §5 to `af-prd`'s shape, asking the questions that
  change the shape of the work in batches of three or four;
- **absent** — run `speca --list` and ask which draft package to finish, or
  which PRD to turn into a package.

Say in one line, once, that `spec <prd>` does the whole of this run
unattended where it is installed and a model credential is set, and that here
every artifact is written in this session instead, in view. Then get on with
it. Do not run `spec`: it would write a second package beside the one you are
writing.

A PRD that is already the `source` of a package in the spec root has been
specified. Name that package and ask what this one changes before writing
anything.

## 2. Orient

Before writing anything, find out what exists. This is the step that decides
whether the package's ids, names and dependencies are real.

```sh
speca --list --all > "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/af-spec-list.json"
```

Every package under the spec root, archived ones included, each with its
`spec_id`, `spec_name`, `status`, verdict and task counts. From it:

- **the number to use** (§3) and the names already taken;
- **the packages this one touches.** For each, `specr <NN> --section prd` for
  what it promises and excludes, and `specr <NN> --section requirements` for
  its glossary and its **execution-path actors**. Both matter later: a
  glossary term this package redefines is a warning across specs, and an
  actor it fails to share with a dependency is an error no later artifact can
  repair (§6);
- **the intent already specified.** A `sealed` or `active` package whose
  intent covers this PRD means the work is done or in progress. Name it and
  ask what this package changes; a change to an active package's intent is a
  new package that supersedes it, never an edit to that one.

Then read the repository, read-only, over the files `git ls-files` lists:

- `README.md`, the docs and the ADRs, for the vocabulary and the decisions
  already made; the errata where the repository keeps them;
- **where things live**: the commands, modules, files and types the behaviour
  touches, with their paths, and what each does today. Every name the package
  writes into `touches`, `pseudocode` or a criterion comes from here;
- **what already exists**: the part of the PRD the code already does. It is
  either out of scope or it is what changes;
- **the checks**: the project's real test and lint commands, from its
  `Makefile`, `package.json`, `pyproject.toml` or CI config. They become
  `test_commands` in §8, and a command from another ecosystem is a defect in
  the package;
- **the external API**: for every library the work calls, the real signatures
  of the symbols it will call, read where they are defined (`go doc
  <pkg>.<Symbol>`, the installed package's source, the lockfile for the
  version). `spec`'s own phases cannot read outside the repository and have
  to mark those signatures unverified; you usually can, and a signature you
  read is one the package carries as verified (§6).

Note the project's language and its stub marker (`panic("not implemented")`,
`TODO`, `NotImplementedError`, `todo!()`): the integration task's steps name
them in §8.

## 3. Reserve the number, the name and the directory

- **The number** is the highest numeric prefix in the spec root **and under
  `archive/`**, plus one, as a string zero-padded to two digits — three once
  past 99, so the directories keep sorting lexically. An archived package
  keeps its number; reusing it would make two packages answer to one id.
- **The name** is `snake_case`, derived from the PRD title:
  `^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(_[a-z0-9]+)*$`. No uppercase, no hyphen, no double or
  trailing underscore. A name the person gives wins.
- **The directory** is `<spec root>/{NN}_{name}`. The spec root is
  `$AF_SPEC_DIR` where it is set, else `.specs/`; where the project keeps it
  somewhere else, every `speca` and `specr` call below takes `--specs-dir`.
  `spec_id` and `spec_name` must agree with the two halves of the directory
  name in all four files (rule C1), so settle both before you write a line.

Create the directory and write nothing else into it: a package holds the four
artifacts, `architecture.md` when there is one, and nothing more — no session
file, no notes, no scratch copies.

## 4. Size the work, and split before you write

A package is **one cohesive feature**: at most 10 requirements, at most 8
criteria each, at most 12 tasks. The validator warns past those; the warnings
are a sign the work was not split, not a formatting nit.

Count the areas of the PRD's `## Requirements`. If the work is plainly
larger — three or more areas that could be built and tested on their own, or
unrelated stories serving different actors — do not write an oversized
package. Propose the split: every scope in order, each with the `snake_case`
name its package will carry and a sentence of what it covers, the
**foundational one first**, and ask which to write now. Then write that scope
alone, name the later scopes in the PRD's `## Non-goals`, and say in the
report which remain and that each is its own package, written by its own run
of this skill (`spec` splits a large PRD by itself; here the person chooses).

## 5. `prd.md`

The package's PRD is the input PRD's body with frontmatter the format
defines. Write the frontmatter exactly in this order and spelling:

```yaml
---
spec_id: "17"
spec_name: "indexed_code_search"
title: "Search the repository by symbol from the shell"
status: "draft"
created_at: "2026-10-27T09:12:44Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-27T09:12:44Z"
intent_hash: null
schema_version: 2
source: "docs/prd/03-search-the-repository-by-symbol.md"
---
```

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `spec_id`, `spec_name` | the two halves of the directory name, quoted strings; `spec_id` keeps its leading zero |
| `title` | the PRD's title, an imperative verb phrase |
| `status` | `"draft"`. §11 changes it, through `speca` |
| `created_at`, `updated_at` | the same ISO 8601 UTC instant, now |
| `intent_hash` | `null`. Activation computes it; a hash you write by hand is one the library will refuse |
| `schema_version` | `2` |
| `source` | where the idea came from: the PRD's path, the issue URL, or `"interactive"` when the person typed it. It is the first question anyone asks of a package |
| `owner`, `supersedes`, `tags` | optional; omit them rather than writing empty values. `supersedes` carries the spec ids this package replaces |

Then the body, which is the PRD's own text: `# {title}`, then `## Intent`,
then the rest. Two rules about it:

- **`## Intent` is required**, spelled exactly so, on a line of its own. It
  is the only machine-read section: activation hashes its text, every later
  write of the package is refused while the section no longer matches the
  hash, and a package without it cannot be activated at all. One short
  paragraph of stable purpose, not a summary of the current plan.
- **No frontmatter inside the body**, and nothing architectural. Design
  belongs in `architecture.md` (§9); behaviour belongs in the requirements.

Keep the PRD's sections as `af-prd` writes them — `## Intent`, `## Goals`,
`## Non-goals`, `## Background`, `## Requirements`, `## Design Decisions`,
`## Dependencies`, `## Verified External API`. Where this run settled a
question the PRD left open, add it to `## Design Decisions`, numbered, with
one line of why: the PRD in the package is the record of what was decided,
and a question answered in your head is a question the next reader asks
again.

Now write the three JSON files as a scaffold — the identity fields with empty
bodies — so the package loads from here on:

```json
{ "$schema": "https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/requirements.v2.json", "spec_id": "17", "spec_name": "indexed_code_search", "schema_version": 2,
  "introduction": "…", "glossary": {}, "requirements": [], "execution_paths": [] }
```

and the same identity in `test_spec.json` (`"tests": []`) and `tasks.json`
(`test_commands`, `"dependencies": []`, `"tasks": []`), each with its own
`$schema` URI — `https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/test_spec.v2.json` and
`https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/tasks.v2.json`. `spec_id`, `spec_name` and
`schema_version` are identical in all three files and agree with `prd.md` and
the directory name; `$schema` is the one of the file it heads.

`speca <NN> --validate` on a scaffold answers with exactly one error,
`completeness`: "spec is an empty scaffold". That is the gate for §5 — the
four files parse, the frontmatter is readable and the package resolves by its
number. Any other error, or a load failure (exit 1), is a defect in what you
just wrote; fix it before writing content. While the package is a scaffold
nothing else is checked, so do not read that one error as a clean bill.

Encode every JSON file with two-space indentation, the fields in the order
the shapes below give them, and a trailing newline. That is the library's own
encoding, and §11 rewrites all four files in it anyway.

## 6. `requirements.json`

What the system must do: one flat list of criteria per requirement, plus the
end-to-end execution paths. Everything comes from the PRD; nothing comes from
your view of how it should be built.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/requirements.v2.json",
  "spec_id": "07",
  "spec_name": "recipe_catalog",
  "schema_version": 2,
  "introduction": "The recipe catalog stores user-created recipes and their ingredients.",
  "glossary": { "recipe": "A named collection of ingredients and preparation steps in the catalog." },
  "requirements": [
    {
      "id": "07-REQ-1",
      "title": "Recipe creation",
      "rationale": "A cook who cannot save a recipe has no reason to open the app twice.",
      "criteria": [
        { "id": "07-REQ-1.1", "pattern": "event_driven",
          "condition": "a client submits POST /recipes with a body carrying a name and at least one ingredient",
          "system": "recipe catalog",
          "action": "persist the recipe and return its assigned identifier",
          "contract": "HTTP 201 with body {id: string, name: string}" },
        { "id": "07-REQ-1.2", "pattern": "unwanted",
          "condition": "the submitted body has no name field",
          "system": "recipe catalog",
          "action": "reject the request and persist nothing",
          "contract": "HTTP 400 with body {error: string}; the catalog row count is unchanged" },
        { "id": "07-REQ-1.3", "pattern": "ubiquitous",
          "system": "recipe catalog",
          "action": "for any persisted recipe, return the same identifier for every subsequent read of it" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "execution_paths": [
    {
      "id": "07-PATH-1",
      "title": "A cook saves a recipe and reads it back",
      "steps": [
        { "actor": "cook", "action": "submits POST /recipes with a recipe body" },
        { "actor": "recipe catalog", "action": "validates the body and writes a row" },
        { "actor": "recipe catalog", "action": "returns 201 with the assigned identifier" },
        { "actor": "cook", "action": "reads GET /recipes/{id} and receives the saved recipe" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The example is the shape `spec`'s own prompt carries; the domain is yours.

**Ids.** `{spec_id}-REQ-{N}` for a requirement, `{spec_id}-REQ-{N}.{C}` for a
criterion with C restarting at 1 inside each requirement,
`{spec_id}-PATH-{N}` for a path. N and C are sequential from 1. Every id
carries this package's `spec_id` and is unique in its family (rule C2).

**The criterion is one EARS sentence in fields.** Choose the pattern first,
then fill only the fields it allows — the schema enforces the combination,
and it reports a wrong one as `'not' failed` on that criterion, which means
`condition` or `guard` is present where the pattern forbids it or absent
where it requires it.

| `pattern` | `condition` | `guard` | Rendered sentence |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ubiquitous` | absent | absent | THE {system} SHALL {action} |
| `event_driven` | the trigger | absent | WHEN {condition}, THE {system} SHALL {action} |
| `complex_event` | the trigger | required | WHEN {condition} AND {guard}, THE {system} SHALL {action} |
| `state_driven` | the state | absent | WHILE {condition}, THE {system} SHALL {action} |
| `unwanted` | the error case | absent | IF {condition}, THEN THE {system} SHALL {action} |
| `optional` | the feature | absent | WHERE {condition}, THE {system} SHALL {action} |

`action` is required and is what the system must do, testably. `system` is
optional and names the component that acts. `contract` is what the caller
observes — the return value, the exit code, the status code, the emitted
event — **required for every `unwanted` criterion** (rule C10) and expected
wherever something else consumes the result. "It reports an error" is not a
contract; "exit code 2 and nothing written to stdout" is.

**There are no other sections.** An edge case is a criterion, usually
`unwanted` or `complex_event`. A correctness property is a `ubiquitous`
criterion whose action begins "for any …". Error handling is an `unwanted`
criterion with a contract. There is no `edge_cases`, no
`correctness_properties`, no `error_handling` and no `user_story`: a
requirement carries an optional one-sentence `rationale` instead.

**For every requirement, consider** — and write a criterion where it
applies: empty or null input; boundary values, with numbers; the operation
failing; authorization failing; a concurrent operation. For anything that
spawns processes, loops, retries or calls a service, also: a timeout,
resource cleanup on failure, an iteration cap, and that library code returns
an error instead of terminating the process.

**Language.** Prefer a measurable constraint to an adjective. The validator
warns on `appropriate`, `properly`, `correctly`, `reasonable`, `relevant`,
`adequate`, `suitable`, `as needed`, `if necessary` and `etc` — each of them
makes a criterion untestable, so say what the word stands for.

**The glossary is an object**, term → definition, for project-specific
vocabulary a newcomer would not know. Not every identifier: it is
documentation, and the validator only warns about a backticked term used in
three or more criteria with no entry. A term an existing package defines
keeps that definition.

**Execution paths** are the end-to-end scenarios, at least one, each with at
least two `{actor, action}` steps. A path starts at a user action, a CLI
command, an API call or a scheduled trigger, ends at a concrete side effect,
and uses logical actors. Each one becomes a smoke test in §7 and work in the
integration task in §8, so write paths you can watch happen.

**Paths across packages.** For every package this one depends on, at least
one step of this package's paths must be played by an actor that also plays a
step in one of that package's paths — the same name, ignoring case. A
dependency with no shared actor is an **error** across specs, and no later
artifact can repair it: decide it here, with the actors you read in §2, and
name a component the other package owns as that package names it.

**`external_apis`** is optional and holds the signatures the work relies on:
`{package, version, verified, symbols: [{name, import_path, signature}]}`.
Set `verified` to `true` only for a symbol whose source you read in §2;
otherwise `false`, with the signature the PRD implies. Never write
"unverified" into `version` or a note — the flag is the record, and `af-impl`
reads it to know what to confirm before use.

Write the file, then take the verdict: `speca <NN> --validate`. With
`test_spec.json` and `tasks.json` still empty you will see two expected
errors — `at '/tests': minItems: got 0, want 1` and `at '/tasks': minItems:
got 0, want 2`. Every other error is yours to fix now. Note that the
cross-file rules C1–C11 do **not** run while any file fails its schema, so
this gate checks the requirements' shape, not yet their coverage.

## 7. `test_spec.json`

How each criterion is verified: **one flat list** of tests, written against
the complete requirements artifact — its real ids, never an invented one.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/test_spec.v2.json",
  "spec_id": "07",
  "spec_name": "recipe_catalog",
  "schema_version": 2,
  "tests": [
    { "id": "TS-07-1", "kind": "unit", "verifies": ["07-REQ-1.1"],
      "title": "A valid recipe body is persisted and its identifier returned",
      "given": ["an empty catalog"],
      "when": "POST /recipes is called with a name and one ingredient",
      "then": ["the status is 201", "the body carries a non-empty id", "the catalog holds one row"],
      "pseudocode": "in http/handlers_test.go: r = post('/recipes', body); assert r.status == 201; assert count(catalog) == 1" },
    { "id": "TS-07-4", "kind": "smoke", "verifies": ["07-PATH-1"],
      "title": "A cook saves a recipe and reads it back through the running service",
      "given": ["the service is running against a real database"],
      "when": "a recipe is posted and then fetched by its returned identifier",
      "then": ["the fetch returns the recipe that was posted"],
      "pseudocode": "extends the end-to-end test in http/server_test.go: id = post('/recipes', rec).json.id; assert get('/recipes/' + id).json == rec",
      "real_components": ["HTTP handler", "recipe catalog", "database"] }
  ]
}
```

The example shows two of the four tests this package needs; `TS-07-2` and
`TS-07-3`, which verify the other two criteria, are left out for length. In
the file the series has no gaps.

**Fields.** `id` is `TS-{spec_id}-{N}`, N sequential from 1 — **one number
series for all four kinds**, with no `E`, `P` or `SMOKE` variant. `kind` is
`unit` (one component, collaborators may be stubbed), `integration` (two or
more real components, only external I/O stubbed), `property` (the generator
in `when`, the invariant in `then`) or `smoke` (one full execution path with
real components). `verifies` is non-empty: criterion ids for `unit`,
`integration` and `property`, a path id for `smoke`, which may also list
criterion ids. `title` says what is verified as a caller sees it. `given` may
be empty; `when` is the action or input; `then` is non-empty and observable.
`pseudocode` is optional, language-agnostic, and may name concrete functions
and files. `real_components` is required and non-empty for `smoke` and
**must be absent for every other kind** (rule C11).

**Coverage, which the format enforces.** Every criterion is in the `verifies`
of at least one test (C4). Every path is in the `verifies` of at least one
`smoke` test, and every `smoke` test verifies at least one path (C5). Every
id in `verifies` resolves in this package (C3). There is no `coverage`
object: coverage is computed, never authored.

**One test per behaviour.** A behaviour is one thing a caller can observe: a
request answered with a status and a body, a store that refuses a duplicate,
a command that exits with a code and writes a file.

- A test lists every criterion its behaviour satisfies, up to four; more is
  usually two behaviours, and the validator warns past four.
- **Variants are a table.** Two tests whose `then` differ only in the input
  are one test over a table: name the cases in `when` and their outcomes in
  `then`.
- A test whose `then` only restates another's is a duplicate: drop it and add
  its criteria to the other's `verifies`.
- For every `unwanted` criterion, assert the caller-observable outcome its
  `contract` names, not that "an error occurred".
- A `property` test is only for an invariant a table of representative
  inputs cannot establish — a round trip, idempotence, an algebraic law. A
  "for any …" criterion is otherwise a `unit` test over such a table, its
  boundaries among them.
- A `smoke` test proves the wiring of its path: it runs the path once with
  its `real_components` and asserts the end state, without asserting again
  what the unit tests verify. One per path, and its `verifies` lists the
  path alone.
- **No artifact tests.** Nothing checks that a struct has a field, a constant
  has a value, a document mentions something, an import graph holds or a
  helper exists.
- **Look at the tests the repository already has** for the code each test
  exercises. Where one already verifies the behaviour, keep the entry — its
  criteria still need a test — and name that test's file and name in
  `pseudocode`, so `af-impl` extends it instead of writing the behaviour
  again under a new name.

The ids stay in this file. `af-impl` writes each test into the test file of
the code it exercises, named for the behaviour it checks
(`TestCreate_RejectsDuplicateSlug`), and records the map from test id to test
in the commit. Write `title` and `pseudocode` so that is possible: say the
behaviour, name the file, never the id.

Take the verdict again. Only the `tasks.json` `minItems` error should remain
beside anything the tests broke.

## 8. `tasks.json`

What work to do, in what order: **one flat, ordered list**. A task is the
unit of dispatch — one coder session, one task. Write it against the complete
requirements and the complete test list.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://agent-fox.dev/schemas/tasks.v2.json",
  "spec_id": "07",
  "spec_name": "recipe_catalog",
  "schema_version": 2,
  "test_commands": { "all_tests": "go test ./... -count=1", "linter": "go vet ./...", "spec_tests": "go test ./catalog/... -count=1" },
  "dependencies": [ { "spec": "06", "reason": "uses the HTTP router and the database handle it sets up" } ],
  "tasks": [
    { "id": 1, "kind": "implement", "title": "Persist and read back recipes",
      "criteria": ["07-REQ-1"], "tests": ["TS-07-1", "TS-07-2", "TS-07-3"],
      "steps": [
        "Add the task's behaviours to catalog/store_test.go and http/handlers_test.go and confirm they fail",
        "Add the recipe row type and the catalog Insert and Get methods",
        "Wire POST /recipes to Insert; return 201 with the assigned id",
        "Reject a body with no name with 400 and an error string, writing nothing"
      ],
      "touches": ["catalog/store.go", "catalog/store_test.go", "http/handlers.go", "http/handlers_test.go"],
      "state": "pending" },
    { "id": 2, "kind": "integration", "title": "Verify the save-and-read path end to end",
      "criteria": [], "tests": ["TS-07-4"],
      "steps": [
        "Extend the end-to-end test in http/server_test.go to save a recipe and read it back against the running service and a real database, and confirm it passes",
        "Trace 07-PATH-1 from the HTTP handler through the catalog to the database; confirm each step calls the next",
        "Confirm the identifier Insert returns is consumed by the handler in production code, not only in tests",
        "Grep catalog/ and http/ for TODO and panic(\"not implemented\") and remove any that remain"
      ],
      "touches": ["http/server_test.go"], "depends_on": [1],
      "done_when": ["`go test ./... -count=1` passes against a real database"],
      "state": "pending" }
  ]
}
```

**Fields.** `id` is an integer from 1, and array order is the default
execution order. `kind` is `implement` or `integration`. `title` is
imperative and specific. `criteria` holds requirement or criterion ids —
non-empty for `implement`, where a requirement id means all its criteria; it
may be empty for `integration`. `tests` is non-empty. `steps` is non-empty,
in order, and concrete enough that a coder who sees only this task, its
criteria and its tests needs nothing else. `touches` is optional: the files,
packages and modules the task creates or changes, the test files it extends
among them. `depends_on` is optional, references lower ids, and absent means
"the previous task" — use it to let independent tasks run in parallel.
`done_when` is optional, for checks beyond the implicit ones. `state` is
`pending` for every task of a new plan. `optional` defaults to false.

Every task carries an implicit definition of done you do not write out: the
listed tests exist, are executable and pass; `all_tests` passes; `linter`
passes. The coder works test-first, so **there are no "write the tests"
tasks** — tests are written inside the task that owns them, into the test
files of the code in `touches`. Write steps that say the behaviours to add,
not the test ids to write.

**`test_commands`** are the project's real commands, from §2: `all_tests`
and `linter` are required, `spec_tests` is optional and narrows the run to
this package's area. A default from another ecosystem makes the plan wrong
for the repository it will be run in.

**`dependencies`** lists the packages the PRD's `## Dependencies` names, each
`{spec, reason}`, where `spec` is the other package's **number alone**
(`"06"`, never `06_router` or its name). A number that names no package in
the spec root makes the package invalid, and so does a dependency whose paths
share no actor with this one (§6).

**Rules that make the plan invalid.** Every test in `test_spec.json` is
listed in the `tests` of at least one task (C7). Every criterion is covered
by the `criteria` of at least one **`implement`** task, directly or through
its requirement id (C8). Every id in `criteria`, `tests` and `depends_on`
resolves, and `depends_on` points at lower ids (C6). Exactly one task has
kind `integration`, it is last, and its `tests` include **every** `smoke`
test (C9).

**Check ownership before you take the verdict.** Go through the test list and
write each test id against the task whose `tests` holds it; then through the
requirements and each criterion id against the `implement` task whose
`criteria` holds it. An id with no owner is the commonest way a plan ships
with a hole. Criteria belong to implement tasks; the integration task owns
the smoke tests.

Keep a task to at most 10 tests and 12 steps, and the plan to at most 12
tasks. **Group by requirement, not by layer**: a task delivers one or two
requirements end to end — data, logic, interface and tests — so each one
leaves the system working.

**The integration task** is last and exists to catch the wiring gaps
component tests cannot see. Its steps cover, in the project's own vocabulary:
trace every execution path through the real code and confirm each step calls
the next with no stub left; for every criterion with a `contract`, confirm a
caller in production code consumes the producer's result, not only a test;
search the files every task's `touches` lists for the project's stub marker
and the common ones; and for any path whose entry point belongs to another
package, confirm that entry point is called from production code. An
execution path that is not live in production code fails this task, and no
erratum satisfies it.

## 9. `architecture.md` (optional)

Write it only when the person asks, or when the PRD carries design detail
that is neither intent nor a criterion: modules and their responsibilities,
the interfaces between them with real signatures, the data models, the
technology choices and their reasons. Sections: `# Architecture: {spec_name}`,
`## Overview`, `## Architecture` (a Mermaid flowchart, then one line of
responsibility per module), `## Components and Interfaces`, `## Data
Models`, `## Technology Stack`, `## Definition of Done`.

Two constraints. It fits the code that exists — the project's module names,
vocabulary and libraries. And **nothing in it is load-bearing**: a renderer
working to a token budget drops it first, so every fact a coder needs to
implement belongs in a criterion or a task step. It has no frontmatter and is
not validated.

## 10. The verdict

```sh
speca <NN> --validate
```

This is the format's whole verdict: the four schemas, the cross-file rules
C1–C11, and the rules across packages. Read `result.validation.errors` — each
entry names its `check`, the `artifact` and the `entity`, and most carry the
repair in the message. `result.traceability` gives the criteria and paths
covered, and `result.valid` is the answer. Exit code 0 reports a verdict,
valid or not; exit 1 means the package could not be **read** at all (a
missing file, malformed JSON or YAML — `error.message` says which) and exit 2
is a reference that names no package.

Work in that order, because the stages gate each other:

1. **`completeness`** — "an empty scaffold" while all three bodies are still
   empty (§5), or "incomplete: <files>" while one of the three is missing.
   A `schema_version` that is not `2` is refused here too, before anything
   else is read.
2. **`json_schema`** — a missing or misspelled field, a wrong type, an
   unknown key, a value outside an enum, `minItems` on a list you left empty.
   `'not' failed` on a criterion is the EARS combination of §6. The
   cross-file rules do not run until every file passes its schema, so a
   schema-clean verdict can still uncover a dozen C-rule errors.
3. **`C1`–`C11`** — the integrity of the ids and the coverage, in the package.
4. **`cross_spec_*`** — an unknown dependency, a cycle, a dependency with no
   actor in common. These need the other packages and are **not** checked by
   anything else, so this is the only call that catches them.

Fix, ask again, and do not go on until `valid` is true. Then read the
warnings: they never block, and three of them are worth acting on anyway.
`vague_language` names the criterion and the word it cannot be tested
through. `missing_contract` names a criterion that describes an error outcome
and has no contract; give it one. `scope_limit` means the work wanted a split
(§4). Say in the report which warnings you left, and why.

Then read the package back as a coder will receive it:

```sh
specr <NN> --markdown-file "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/af-spec-<NN>.md" > "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/af-spec-<NN>.json"
```

Both files go outside the repository, so the tree stays clean, and stdout
goes to a file because the envelope repeats the whole Markdown.

Read that Markdown, not your own drafts. It renders every criterion as its
EARS sentence with its contract, each test under what it verifies, and each
task with what it owns — which is where a criterion that reads as two
behaviours, a task with no steps a stranger could follow, or a test that
restates another becomes obvious. Fix what you find and validate again.

Without `speca` and `specr` installed, validate against the schemas in
`specification/schemas/*.v2.json` of a `spec` checkout with any JSON Schema
validator, check C1–C11 and the actor rule by hand from §6 to §8, leave the
package in `draft`, and say in the report that no verdict was taken.

## 11. Activate

A package that validates is activated; one that does not stays in `draft`
with its errors named in the report. Activation is a lifecycle move, so it
goes through `speca`, never through an edit of the frontmatter:

```sh
speca <NN> --status active
```

It computes the hash of the `## Intent` section, writes it into
`intent_hash`, and rewrites **all four artifacts** in the library's canonical
encoding — which is also what normalises anything your hand-written files
spelled differently. Three things to know about it. It refuses a package with
no `## Intent` to hash, and one that fails the package's **own** rules
(`--force` overrides the second; this run does not use it). It does **not**
apply the rules across packages, so it will activate a package with an
unknown dependency that `speca --validate` rejects — which is why the verdict
comes first, always. And the files it writes are left with mode `0600`,
because it writes them through private temporary files; `chmod` them to the
repository's usual mode before committing where that matters.

After activation the `## Intent` section is closed: every write of the
package is refused while its text no longer hashes to `intent_hash`. Changing
the intent then means a new package that supersedes this one — so if the
person is still weighing the intent, leave the package in `draft`, say so,
and give them the one call that activates it.

Leave the package in `draft` when the person asks (`spec --no-activate` does
the same), and when the verdict is not clean.

Commit as the repository's instructions say — package and PRD together, on a
branch of its own, where they call for one. Where they say nothing, leave the
files uncommitted and say so.

## Never

- **Write the code.** This run writes a specification, not an
  implementation. No source file, no test file, no fix "while here".
  `af-impl` implements the package.
- **Touch another package.** No edit, no state change, no renumbering. A
  change to an active package's intent is a new package that supersedes it.
- **Hand-edit the lifecycle.** `status` and `intent_hash` change through
  `speca --status`, and a task's state through `speca --task`. A hash or a
  status written by hand is drift the library refuses.
- **Write derived or v1 structures.** No `coverage`, no `traceability`, no
  task groups, no subtasks, no `edge_cases`, `correctness_properties`,
  `error_handling` or `user_story`, no `TS-E`/`TS-P`/`TS-SMOKE` id variants,
  no `_session.json` or any other file in the package directory.
- **Invent an id, a name or a signature.** Every id carries this package's
  prefix and resolves; every file, command and module the package names
  exists under that name or is said to be new; a signature nobody read is
  `verified: false`.
- **Put design in the PRD, or behaviour only in `architecture.md`.** The PRD
  is intent and behaviour; the criteria are the contract; the architecture is
  optional and load-bearing for nothing.
- **Leave a question open.** A package is the input to an unattended coder.
  Decide it with the person, or decide it from the code and record it in
  `## Design Decisions`.
- **Run `spec`.** It writes a package of its own beside yours. `speca` and
  `specr` are the commands this run uses.
- **Activate on an unclean verdict**, or activate a package whose intent the
  person is still weighing.
- **Reuse an archived number**, or renumber a package to close a gap.

## 12. Report

- **The package**: its directory, `spec_id`, `spec_name`, title, status, and
  the artifacts written.
- **The counts**: requirements, criteria, execution paths, tests by kind,
  tasks, and the criteria and paths covered, from `speca`'s `traceability`.
- **The verdict**: `valid`, and where it is false every error by its rule.
  Then the warnings you left, each with why.
- **The decisions** this run made that the PRD had left open, and anything
  the person settled.
- **The dependencies**: each package named, with its status, and any that is
  neither sealed nor has its integration task done — `af-impl` refuses to
  implement before such a package, so it belongs in the report, not in a
  footnote.
- **The external API**: what was verified, and what is `verified: false` for
  `af-impl` to confirm.
- **The split**, where §4 found one: the scopes that remain, in order, and
  that each is its own package.
- **The next steps**:

  ```sh
  speca <NN>                   # the package's state, its next task and its verdict
  speca <NN> --status active   # activate it, where it was left in draft
  specr <NN>                   # read it as a coder receives it
  /af-impl <NN>                # implement it
  ```
